<seg id="s0003" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">The diagnosis of SPS was further supported by a sub-
sequent serum anti-glutamic acid decarboxylase antibody
(GAD ab) > 2000 units/mL (reference range <10 units/
mL). Lumbar puncture was also performed, demonstrat-
ing an elevated protein of 0.82 g/L (reference range 0.15–
0.45 g/L). His cerebrospinal ﬂuid (CSF) immunoglobulin
G (IgG) was 0.06 g/L, with a normal IgG/albumin ratio of
0.12 (reference range <0.25), and normal CSF oligoclonal
bands and glucose.
He was trialled with 4 days of 1 g methylprednisolone
without effect. He was then given intravenous immuno-
globulin (IVIG) 0.4 g/kg/day for 5 days (total 51 g/day).
Clonazepam and baclofen were slowly up-titrated to
0.5 mg t.d.s. and 15 mg t.d.s., respectively, over 2 weeks.
His spasticity was signiﬁcantly reduced and his mobility
improved to a four-wheel walker without assistance. He
was transferred to a rehabilitation hospital, and dis-
charged home 2 months later.
He suffered a mild relapse of leg spasticity 3 weeks
post-discharge, but responded well to prednisone 50 mg
daily and repeat IVIG infusion. He remains able to mobi-
lise using a single point stick, with only mild right leg
stiffness and no further facial spasms. Azathioprine was
slowly introduced to 100 mg daily, and his prednisone
was weaned to 25 mg alternate days, with continued
monthly IVIG infusions.
SPS is an uncommon yet under-diagnosed autoim-
mune neuromuscular disorder,
1 ﬁrst described in 1956 by
Moersch and Woltman.2 It is characterised by truncal and
proximal limb muscle rigidity and superimposed episodic
spasms, often associated with other autoimmune dis-
eases, malignancies and anxiety disorders.
1,3–5 The epide-
miology of SPS is unknown, but appears to affect women
more than men between ages 30 and 70,
5 with 50 being
the typical age of onset. 6
The tonic spasticity of SPS begins insidiously over
months in the thoraco-lumbar paraspinal and proximal
leg muscles to cause hyperlordosis and progressive gait
impairment, resulting in falls, task-speciﬁc phobias and
secondary anxiety disorders.
1,3–5 Continuous, simulta-
neous contractions of both agonist and antagonist muscle
groups are seen under electromyography (EMG).
3
The episodic spasms of SPS begin with abrupt, painful,
bilateral jerks of various muscle groups, which are fol-
lowed by a sustained tonic activity that resolves over
seconds to minutes.
1,3,6 These myoclonic jerks are typi-
cally precipitated by unexpected auditory, tactile or emo-
tional stimuli.
1,3,6
SPS may be under-recognised because muscle stiffness,
spasms and cramps are common, and anxiety and emo-
tional components may be dismissed as primary psychi-
atric illness.
4 Therefore the diagnosis of SPS requires
awareness and high index of suspicion.1 Differential diag-
nosis of SPS includes tetanus, ﬁbromyalgia, dystonias,
various myelopathies and less commonly, myotonia and
neuromyotonia,
3,6 which affect distal more than proximal
muscles, and have distinguishing characteristic EMG
ﬁndings.
6 Startle myoclonus and the characteristic EMG
ﬁndings are helpful in distinguishing SPS from other
differentials. High serum and/or CSF GAD antibodies
further support the diagnosis.
The autoimmune aetiology of SPS was established in
1990 by the detection of grossly elevated GAD abs in the
serum and CSF of SPS patients.
7 Blood and CSF GAD abs
are present in 60–90% of classic SPS patients.3,5,6 GAD abs
block the conversion of glutamate to the major inhibitory</seg>
<seg id="s0004" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">neurotransmitter gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA).
Marked intrathecal antibody response in SPS is hypoth-
esised to block the action of the GAD-65 isoform that
regulates short-term demands for GABA in the spinal
grey matter interneurons and intra-cortical inhibitory
neurones without structural damage.
3 This causes defec-
tive descending inhibitory spinal modulation, resulting in
the unique syndrome of chronic tetany and spasmodic
reﬂex myoclonus from enhanced spinal interneurone
excitability.
6 Low cortical GABA production may also
lower the threshold for anxiety disorders 4 and seizures.
GAD abs are also found in other diseases, including
type 1 diabetes mellitus (T1DM). However, as demon-
strated in our patient, SPS patients have at least 50 times
higher than baseline serum GAD ab titres (7–215
micrograms/mL) than in T1DM (10 times the baseline;
200–1760 ng/mL),
1,3 and appear to recognise different
epitopes.1,3,5 GAD ab level monitoring is not clinically
useful in SPS.1
Three major subsets of SPS are recognised: autoim-
mune, paraneoplastic and idiopathic. 5 The commonest
autoimmune variants are associated with pernicious
anaemia (5%), thyroid disease (10%) and T1DM (30–
60%).
6 The paraneoplastic variant occurs in 5% of SPS, 1
and should be suspected with prominent upper limb and
neck spasticity, especially when unilateral.
6 Amphiphysin
and gephyrin antibodies are more commonly found, with
or without GAD abs.
1
Other clinically distinct SPS-plus syndromes include
stiff-limb syndrome, jerking stiff-person syndrome
and progressive encephalomyelitis with rigidity and
myoclonus.
1,3,5,6,8
Reﬂecting the intrathecal de novo synthesis of antibod-
ies, the CSF in SPS is usually abnormal, as in our patient.
Oligoclonal IgG bands are detected in 60% of cases,
whereas the cell count, total protein and IgG levels are
less frequently elevated.
6 EMG shows a background of
physiological continuous motor activity with a unique,
stereotyped recruitment order of muscles along the axial
neuroaxis during spasmodic reﬂex myoclonus.
3,5,6</seg>
