The two main goals of treatment are to enhance
GABA neurotransmission and immunomodulation.
Long-acting benzodiazepines (diazepam and clon-
azepam) and baclofen diminish the severity of spasms
and stiffness,
3 and are the mainstay of treatment. Diaz-
epam dose ranges from 5 to 100 mg/day in divided doses
every 3–6 hours, and baclofen up to 40–60 mg per day in
twice or three times daily doses.
1,5 Intrathecal baclofen
through an implanted pump is effective, but at the risk of
serious complications, including acute withdrawal reac-
tions, autonomic crisis and death.
1,6 Botulinum toxin
injection, dantrolene and antiepileptic drugs are occa-
sionally used.
1,3
Immunomodulation is required for SPS patients with
suboptimal responses to diazepam or baclofen. 5 As our
case demonstrates, oral or intravenous corticosteroids up
to 60 mg/day may be effective in some patients.
5,6
However, IVIG at 2 g/kg given over 3 to 5 days is the
preferred immunotherapy. A randomised controlled trial
with cross-over design demonstrated efﬁcacy of IVIG in
16 SPS patients with GAD abs.
1,3,5 Improvement may last
between 1 and 4 months, and maintenance infusions are
required.1 Plasmaphresis may be effective in up to 40% of
patients as reported in case studies, 1,3 but has variable
response rates.5 Rituximab is promising as a direct anti-
body depleting immunotherapy that results in transient
reductions in intra-thecal GAD antibody levels with sig-
niﬁcant clinical improvements in some case studies.
3,5,9
To our knowledge, only four cases of thymoma-
associated SPS are reported in the literature, often of the
lymphoctyic type B1 or B2 and associated with concur-
rent myasthenia gravis.
10 GAD abs were positive in two
of the four patients; and most SPS symptoms resolved
following thymectomy.
5,10 Rare SPS cases with Hodgkin
lymphoma and one case of diffuse large B cell lym-
phoma of the pituitary have also been described.
10,11
<seg id="s0005" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">However, the combination of non-Hodgkin lymphoma,
thymoma and SPS have not been described to date. This
unusual case of classic SPS is consistent with the
increasingly elucidated antibody pathogenesis of SPS,
and highlights this under-diagnosed and unique disorder
that should be considered in unusual presentations of
myelopathy, back or limb spasms, and/or anxiety/phobia
disorders.</seg>
